The best approach for `File.*` and `Directory.*` is to adopt the **`System.IO.Abstractions`** NuGet package — it's battle-tested and already provides `IFileSystem` with a mock-friendly test helper.

## Setup

**1. Install packages:**
```bash
dotnet add <YourProject> package System.IO.Abstractions
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

**2. Register in DI** (Program.cs / Startup.cs):
```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

**3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes:**
```csharp
using System.IO.Abstractions;

public class MyService(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string ReadConfig(string path)
        => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);

    public void SaveConfig(string path, string content)
        => fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);

    public bool EnsureDirectory(string path)
        => fileSystem.Directory.Exists(path);
}
```

**4. Test with `MockFileSystem`:**
```csharp
using System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers;

var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/config.json", new MockFileData("{\"key\": \"value\"}") }
});

// Directory.Exists works too — mock directories via AddDirectory
mockFs.AddDirectory("/data");

var service = new MyService(mockFs);

Assert.Equal("{\"key\": \"value\"}", service.ReadConfig("/config.json"));
Assert.True(service.EnsureDirectory("/data"));

service.SaveConfig("/output.json", "hello");
Assert.Equal("hello", mockFs.File.ReadAllText("/output.json"));
```

## Why this over a custom wrapper?

| | Custom `IFileWrapper` | `System.IO.Abstractions` |
|---|---|---|
| Coverage | Only what you wrap | Full `File`, `Directory`, `Path`, `Stream`… |
| Maintenance | You own it | Community-maintained, tested |
| Test helpers | You build them | `MockFileSystem` included |

**Key rule:** only wrap what you actually call — `IFileSystem` already does this for you with the full surface area. Avoid rolling your own when an established package exists.

